  "summary": "Folded rawhide suitcases painted in bold geometric fields — the luggage of the buffalo trail.",
  "description": "Parfleche — from French \"parer\" (to deflect) — is untanned rawhide hardened and folded into flat envelopes, cylinders and boxes, then painted in bold two-to-four color geometrics: striped fields, hourglass forms, triangular tipi motifs, each nation and each maker varying the canon. Nomadic peoples used parfleche as trunk, saddlebag, moccasin case and food store, tough enough to trail thousands of miles; women made and painted them, and their designs are signature works of the Plains artistic canon. Museums from the Smithsonian to the Fenimore hold classic 1870s-90s examples; contemporary artists like Niio Perkins and Chelysa Owens-Cyr rework the forms today.",
  "culturalContext": "Parfleche was Plains women's signature art of the horse era — trunk, saddlebag and canvas in folded rawhide — and its painted geometry now anchors museum canons of Plains design; contemporary artists (Owens-Cyr, Perkins) rework the forms, keeping the buffalo-trail luggage a living tradition.",
